<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<!--
  jev-router as a macOS LaunchAgent. `jev-router setup` installs it for you; this template is the
  manual route, and setup writes the same agent from it. It runs "jev-router serve" with the live
  view on port 4100, the config ~/.config/jev-router/config.json and the keys from
  ~/.config/jev-router/env (the arguments are in ProgramArguments below), starts when you log in,
  and restarts the router when it fails. The router listens on http://127.0.0.1:4000 and its live
  view on http://127.0.0.1:4100. To move it, put JEV_ROUTER_HOST or JEV_ROUTER_PORT in the env file, where
  every jev-router command reads them.

  launchd doesn't expand ~ or $HOME, and a LaunchAgent doesn't get your shell's PATH, while
  jev-router starts with "#!/usr/bin/env node". So the install commands fill in two placeholders
  from your shell: @HOME@, your home directory, and @PATH@, the directories that hold jev-router and
  node on your PATH. A "bash -lc" wrapper would expand ~ but still miss node for many setups:
  macOS's login shell is zsh, and nvm and similar version managers set up PATH only in interactive
  shells. Run the install commands again after you reinstall jev-router under another Node.js.

  Install by hand, after "npm install -g github:dirien/jev-router#semver:^1":

    command -v jev-router node                   (both must print a path)
    jev-router init                              (writes ~/.config/jev-router/config.json)
    mkdir -p ~/.config/jev-router ~/Library/Logs/jev-router ~/Library/LaunchAgents
    touch ~/.config/jev-router/env && chmod 600 ~/.config/jev-router/env
    (put your keys in ~/.config/jev-router/env, one KEY=value per line: TYPESAFE_API_KEY, ...)
    plist="$(npm root -g)/@ediri/jev-router/examples/service/launchd/io.github.dirien.jev-router.plist"
    path="$(dirname "$(command -v jev-router)"):$(dirname "$(command -v node)"):/usr/bin:/bin"
    sed -e "s|@HOME@|$HOME|g" -e "s|@PATH@|$path|g" "$plist" > ~/Library/LaunchAgents/io.github.dirien.jev-router.plist
    plutil -lint ~/Library/LaunchAgents/io.github.dirien.jev-router.plist
    launchctl bootstrap gui/$(id -u) ~/Library/LaunchAgents/io.github.dirien.jev-router.plist

  Use:

    launchctl print gui/$(id -u)/io.github.dirien.jev-router | head    (state and PID)
    launchctl kill HUP gui/$(id -u)/io.github.dirien.jev-router        (re-read the config)
    launchctl kickstart -k gui/$(id -u)/io.github.dirien.jev-router    (restart after an upgrade)
    tail -f ~/Library/Logs/jev-router/router.err.log                  (messages and startup errors)
    jev-router report                                                 (requests, spend and savings)

  The router writes its JSON log to ~/.local/state/jev-router/router.log and rotates it at logMaxBytes
  (50 MiB by default); jev-router report and jev-router ui read that file without an argument. launchd
  never rotates files, so it gets only the messages for people, which stay small. ExitTimeOut gives the
  router's 30-second drain room: launchd's default of 20 seconds would cut streams short.

  Uninstall ("jev-router uninstall" does this, and undoes the rest of setup):

    launchctl bootout gui/$(id -u)/io.github.dirien.jev-router
    rm ~/Library/LaunchAgents/io.github.dirien.jev-router.plist
-->
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
  <key>Label</key>
  <string>io.github.dirien.jev-router</string>
  <key>ProgramArguments</key>
  <array>
    <string>/usr/bin/env</string>
    <string>jev-router</string>
    <string>serve</string>
    <string>--ui</string>
    <string>4100</string>
    <string>--config</string>
    <string>@HOME@/.config/jev-router/config.json</string>
    <string>--env-file</string>
    <string>@HOME@/.config/jev-router/env</string>
    <string>--log-file</string>
    <string>@HOME@/.local/state/jev-router/router.log</string>
  </array>
  <key>EnvironmentVariables</key>
  <dict>
    <key>PATH</key>
    <string>@PATH@</string>
  </dict>
  <key>RunAtLoad</key>
  <true/>
  <key>KeepAlive</key>
  <dict>
    <key>SuccessfulExit</key>
    <false/>
  </dict>
  <key>ExitTimeOut</key>
  <integer>35</integer>
  <key>StandardOutPath</key>
  <string>/dev/null</string>
  <key>StandardErrorPath</key>
  <string>@HOME@/Library/Logs/jev-router/router.err.log</string>
</dict>
</plist>
